Article 6 The people's police of public security organs shall, according to their respective functions and duties, perform the following duties according to law:
(1) preventing, stopping, and investigating illegal and criminal activities;
(2) maintaining social public order and stopping acts that endanger social public order;
(3) maintaining traffic safety and traffic order, and handling traffic accidents;
(4) organizing and implementing fire protection work, and exercising fire protection supervision;
(5) managing firearms and ammunition, controlled knives, and dangerous articles such as flammable, explosive, highly toxic, and radioactive substances;
(6) managing special trades as prescribed by laws and regulations;
(7) guarding specific persons designated by the State and guarding important places and facilities;
(8) managing assemblies, processions, and demonstrations;
(9) managing household registration, nationality, exit and entry affairs, and affairs concerning the residence and travel of foreigners within China;
(10) maintaining public order in border areas;
(11) executing criminal punishments imposed on criminals sentenced to criminal detention or deprivation of political rights;
(12) supervising and managing the security protection work of computer information systems;
(13) guiding and supervising the security and protection work of State organs, social organizations, enterprises, institutions, and key construction projects, and guiding the public security prevention work of mass organizations such as public security committees;
(14) other duties as prescribed by laws and regulations.

Article 9 For the purpose of maintaining social public order, the people's police of public security organs may, after producing the relevant certificates, interrogate and inspect persons suspected of illegal or criminal activities on the spot; if, after interrogation and inspection, any of the following circumstances exists, they may take such persons to a public security organ and, with the approval of the said public security organ, continue to interrogate them:
(1) being accused of criminal acts;
(2) being suspected of committing crimes on the spot;
(3) being suspected of committing crimes but having unidentified identity;
(4) carrying articles that are possibly stolen goods.
The period of detention of the interrogated person shall not exceed twenty-four hours from the time he or she is taken to the public security organ; under special circumstances, with the approval of a public security organ at or above the county level, the period may be extended to forty-eight hours, and a record of the interrogation shall be kept. If continued interrogation is approved, the family of the interrogated person or the unit to which he or she belongs shall be notified immediately. If continued interrogation is not approved, the interrogated person shall be released immediately.
If, after continued interrogation, the public security organ considers that the interrogated person needs to be detained or subjected to other compulsory measures according to law, a decision shall be made within the time limit specified in the preceding paragraph; if no such decision can be made within the time limit specified in the preceding paragraph, the interrogated person shall be released immediately.

Article 20 The people's police must:
(1) enforce the law impartially and handle matters fairly;
(2) set an example in observing social ethics;
(3) be courteous to others and perform their duties in a civilized manner;
(4) respect the customs and habits of the people.

Article 22 The people's police shall not commit any of the following acts:
(1) spreading remarks that damage the reputation of the State, joining illegal organizations, participating in activities such as assemblies, processions, or demonstrations aimed at opposing the State, or participating in strikes;
(2) divulging State secrets or secrets concerning police work;
(3) resorting to deception, concealing case facts, or harboring or conniving at illegal or criminal activities;
(4) extorting confessions by torture or physically punishing or maltreating offenders;
(5) illegally depriving or restricting others' personal freedom, or illegally searching others' bodies, articles, residences, or premises;
(6) extorting or demanding or accepting bribes;
(7) beating others or instigating others to beat others;
(8) illegally imposing penalties or collecting fees;
(9) accepting invitations or gifts from parties or their agents;
(10) engaging in for-profit business activities or being employed by any individual or organization;
(11) neglecting duties or failing to perform legal obligations;
(12) other acts that violate the law or discipline.

Article 26 To serve as a people's policeman, a person shall meet the following conditions:
(1) being a citizen who has reached the age of 18;
(2) endorsing the Constitution of the People's Republic of China;
(3) having good political and professional qualities and good conduct;
(4) being in good health;
(5) having an educational level of senior high school graduation or above;
(6) being willing to engage in the work of a people's policeman.
No person shall serve as a people's policeman under any of the following circumstances:
(1) having been subjected to criminal punishment for committing a crime;
(2) having been discharged from public employment.

Article 28 Persons holding leading positions in the people's police shall meet the following conditions:
(1) having knowledge of legal expertise;
(2) having experience in political and legal work and certain abilities in organization, management, and command;
(3) having an educational level of junior college or above;
(4) having been trained in a people's police school and having passed the examination.

Article 35 Whoever refuses or obstructs a people's policeman from performing his or her duties according to law and commits any of the following acts shall be given a public security administration penalty:
(1) publicly insulting a people's policeman who is performing duties;
(2) obstructing a people's policeman from investigating and collecting evidence;
(3) refusing or obstructing a people's policeman from entering relevant residences or premises for tasks such as pursuit, search, or rescue;
(4) intentionally setting up obstacles to police vehicles performing emergency tasks such as rescue, disaster relief, pursuit, or guard;
(5) committing any other act of refusing or obstructing a people's policeman from performing his or her duties.
If the acts prescribed in the preceding paragraph are committed by violence or threat and constitute a crime, criminal liability shall be investigated according to law.

Article 45 When handling a public security case, a people's policeman shall withdraw under any of the following circumstances, and the party or his or her legal representative shall also have the right to request his or her withdrawal:
(1) being a party to the case or a close relative of a party to the case;
(2) having an interest in the case himself or herself or through a close relative;
(3) having any other relationship with a party to the case that may affect the impartial handling of the case.
The withdrawal provided for in the preceding paragraph shall be decided by the relevant public security organ.
The withdrawal of a people's policeman in the process of handling a criminal case shall be governed by the provisions of the Criminal Procedure Law.
